messages:
alice (DevEx): Hey, running into a weird flake when running our unit suite for the JS SDK in Vitest. Tests pass locally with CommonJS but fail when we set "type": "module" in package.json (ESM). Error is: 

Error: SyntaxError: The requested module '/node_modules/@redwood/inference-js/dist/index.js' does not provide an export named 'default'
    at ModuleJob._instantiate (internal/modules/esm/module_job.js:125:21)

Seems related to how our build outputs exports + how vitest transforms node_modules. Any quick ideas?

bryan (SDK): Hmm. Are you importing default or named? Our build has both CJS + ESM entrypoints but the bundler can emit only named exports in some cases. Can you paste the import + package.json?

alice (DevEx): Import: `import Inference from '@redwood/inference-js'`
package.json snippet:
```
{
  "name": "example",
  "type": "module",
  "devDependencies": {
    "vitest": "^0.34.0",
    "esbuild": "^0.18.0"
  }
}
```
Vitest config (vite.config.test.ts):
```
import { defineConfig } from 'vitest/config'
export default defineConfig({
  test: {
    globals: true,
    environment: 'node',
    deps: {
      inline: ['@redwood/inference-js']
    }
  }
})
```
I tried `deps.inline` and `transformMode` but still see the same default export issue.

sara (QA): Also seeing flaky auth/retry unit tests — we mock the token refresh flow which imports runtime-only globals (fetch/crypto) and that collapses under ESM transform. Could be two things colliding.

chris (Eng): Two probable root causes: 1) Our published ESM build uses `export { something }` but not `export default` — consumers doing `import default` will break. 2) Test runner transform might be loading the package as CJS/interop and getting named-exports-only. Quick checks: what does `node -p "require('node:fs').readFileSync('./node_modules/@redwood/inference-js/package.json','utf8')"` show for `exports`/`module`/`main`/`module` fields?

alice (DevEx): package.json from node_modules shows "exports": {"import": "./dist/index.mjs","require": "./dist/index.cjs"} and in `dist/index.mjs` we `export function createClient(){}` and `export default createClient` in rollup output — but the bundler plugin we use sometimes outputs a `default` as `exports.default =` depending on build. Strange.

bryan (SDK): If dist/index.mjs truly has `export default createClient`, import should work. The vitest transform might be rewriting imports. Two workarounds that have helped teams here:
- Use `deps.inline: ['@redwood/inference-js']` (you did) + ensure esbuild target is modern in the vite/vitest config.
- Or import the named export in tests: `import { createClient as Inference } from '@redwood/inference-js'` — ugly but a quick patch.

alice (DevEx): Tried named import, still failing for other tests: `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'webcrypto')` coming from token code that expects `globalThis.crypto.subtle` (edge runtime). In node test environment crypto is not present.

chris (Eng): For edge compat in node test env you can polyfill webcrypto at test startup: create `setupTests.ts` and in vitest config add `setupFiles: ['./setupTests.ts']` with:
```
// setupTests.ts
import { webcrypto } from 'node:crypto'
// some node versions require globalThis.cast
// @ts-ignore
globalThis.crypto = webcrypto
```
That fixes the crypto errors. Also polyfill fetch if needed: `globalThis.fetch = (await import('node-fetch')).default` or use `undici`.

sara (QA): For the auth mocks: we were relying on `vi.mock('@redwood/inference-js/dist/auth', () => ({ refreshToken: vi.fn() }))` which fails under ESM resolution. Switched to `vi.mock('@redwood/inference-js/auth', async () => ({
  refreshToken: async () => 'mock-token'
}))` — using virtual named path that matches exports helped.

alice (DevEx): Ok I applied polyfill + changed tests to named import for problem areas and adjusted vitest to inline deps + explicit esbuild options:
```
test: {
  deps: { inline: ['@redwood/inference-js'] },
  environment: 'node',
  setupFiles: ['./setupTests.ts'],
  esbuild: { target: 'es2020' }
}
```
After that most tests pass but CI still flakes on macOS runners with `Error: The requested module ... does not provide an export named 'default'` in one snapshot test.

bryan (SDK): CI difference suggests published package maybe includes both CJS/ESM and Node loader chooses different path on macOS runners (different node version). Ensure we ship an explicit `exports` map for the package with both `default` and named entrypoints, or better: always include a top-level `index.mjs` that re-exports default as named too: `export { default } from './runtime.mjs'; export { createClient } from './runtime.mjs'`. That guarantees both import styles work.

chris (Eng): Short-term: add a test-time shim that forces the module interop to a consistent shape: in `setupTests.ts`:
```
import * as sdk from '@redwood/inference-js'
// @ts-ignore
if (sdk && !('default' in sdk)) {
  // create a default for CommonJS-style consumers
  // @ts-ignore
  sdk.default = sdk.createClient || (() => {})
}
```
This is gross but will stabilize CI while SDK team patches package exports.

alice (DevEx): Thanks — putting the shim in place now. Filing an SDK issue for a proper exports + default parity: https://internal/track/RED-4567 (added steps + repro).

ci-bot: Deploy check: unit-tests (linux) #42 succeeded :rocket:

alice (DevEx): Follow-ups: Bryan, can you add the re-export stub to next SDK build so tests don't need the shim? Also add a short note in the JS SDK README about test runners + node ESM pitfalls + recommended vitest config.

bryan (SDK): Yep will do. I'll open a PR to add `export { default } from './createClient.mjs'` and update package.json exports. ETA EOD PST.

sara (QA): After the shim CI is stable, I'll re-run flaky auth test suite across mac/linux to validate. :thumbsup:

alice (DevEx): Awesome — thanks all. If anyone else hits weird ESM/edge flakiness ping me and drop the minimal repro (single-file + package.json) here.
